ropilot.description:
Roblox rules:
- Move models with SetPrimaryPartCFrame, not PivotTo, in almost all cases.
- task.cancel can't cancel a task.spawn handle, so never write a = task.spawn(fn); task.cancel(a).
- Read similar code in the project before writing new code.
- Check the UI hierarchy with the Studio tools before referencing UI paths such as buttons and frames.
- Don't hardcode sizes, images, or positions. Use the project's constants and configs.
- Ignore linter errors in files that use class(), which comes from a custom class library.
- Use existing utilities when present: std.SimpleTween, std.FormatTime, std.FormatNumber.
- Read the roblox-tester skill before playtesting.
- Use ropilot_edit_ctx_run_command, which runs Lua in Studio edit mode, only when the user asks you to change or do something in Studio, such as setting Lighting or selected instance properties. Normal coding tasks rarely need it.

If Ropilot key or auth failures occur, ask the user to check their Ropilot key in Ropilot Studio Settings, or refresh their API key on the dashboard: https://ropilot.ai/dashboard.

Inserts an asset into the game by its numeric Roblox asset ID.
Use this tool when you have a specific asset ID to insert directly, rather than searching the Creator Store.
The asset will be loaded, validated, and placed in the scene.
Always provide assetName when you know the name of the asset (e.g. from search results or user request). The inserted instance will be named using assetName — if omitted, it defaults to a generic name.
Supports models, meshes, images/decals, audio, video, animations, and packages.


exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__ropilot__insert_asset(args: {
  // Numeric Roblox asset ID to insert.
  assetId: string;
  // Name for the inserted instance in the game tree. Always provide this when you know the asset name (e.g. from search_asset results). If omitted, defaults to a generic name.
  assetName?: string;
  // Asset type hint. If provided, skips the metadata API lookup and uses this type directly. Use when the caller already knows the asset type (e.g. from inventory search results). 'Image' and 'Decal' both insert as a Decal instance.
  assetType?: "Model" | "Package" | "Mesh" | "MeshPart" | "Image" | "Decal" | "Audio" | "Video" | "Animation";
  // DataModel path to parent the asset under (e.g. game.Workspace.Folder1). Defaults to workspace.
  parentPath?: string;
  // Optional. Omit to use the currently connected Roblox Studio.
  studio_id?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

