Run Python in the user's local Blender, which Ropilot runs headless. It starts on first use with an empty scene (camera and light) and stops after 30 idle minutes, so save anything worth keeping. The first call installs Ropilot's Blender, a one-time ~400 MB download the user sees progress for. If a call says Blender is still installing, wait about 30 seconds and call again. `bpy` is preloaded and variables persist between calls. Returns printed output, the traceback on error, and repr(result) if the code sets `result`. Use bpy.data, bmesh, and operators that need no viewport (bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_*, modifiers, bake, export). view3d and screen operators are unavailable. Save a .blend with bpy.ops.wm.save_as_mainfile(filepath=...) when the user wants one. The user sees a 3D preview after each call. Check your work with ropilot_blender_screenshot, and bring results into Studio with ropilot_blender_import. Costs 0.5 Ropilot Actions per call, so make each call do meaningful work.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__ropilot__ropilot_blender_run_python(args: {
  // Python code to run in Blender
  code: string;
  // Timeout in seconds (default 60, max 600)
  timeout?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

